Expected elements: MISSING — a timeline or scrubber expressing position within the trace.

That is finding #1, it is P1, and the rating is capped at 4. The identity bar shows the stepping toolbar, the phase rail and `128 / 1315`, but nothing that draws position spatially; a desktop CodeTracer user reads a trace position off a track, and at this width there is none.

Register-wise the surface is otherwise convincingly the tool: dark, dense, editor palette in the Code pane, editor-style file tabs, inline value chips (`damage: 0->2000`), and the desktop pane vocabulary intact — CODE, CALL TRACE, EVENT LOG, VALUES, TRANSACTION. The language mark NOIR is correct here.

Where the register slips: the provenance banner opens the flagship tool surface with four lines of proportional marketing-voice body copy on a full-width band — the copy is honest and well written, but its typographic register is the explorer's, and it is the first thing the eye lands on. The Transaction pane then prints a raw wire enum as human copy: "Status reason: private-part-succeeded-public-part-succeeded". Badge primitives disagree across one screen — an outlined blue pill, an outlined orange chip, two filled chips and a bare uppercase mark. And four panes carry three different header treatments.

Highest-priority fixes: set the banner in the tool's own voice and type, and render the status reason as a phrase rather than a wire identifier.

Rating: 4/10

